  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 222 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAs the low in the western United States opens up and fills early \nnext week, the model consensus is now for the trough to deamplify\nquickly and move northeast from Colorado and northern New Mexico\nup toward the Upper Mississippi Valley as the ridge remains\nrelatively strong over the Lower Mississippi Valley. This\ntrajectory of the trough takes much of the precipitation \nnortheast with it. The highest precipitation potential remains in \nnorthwestern Oklahoma early Monday, and although there still is \nsome precipitation potential farther east as the tail end of the \nweakening trough moves through the Central Plains, precipitation \nchances do decrease getting farther east. Will continue to watch\ntrends in the models.\n\nLate in the week, there are significant differences in how the\nweather pattern evolve with the operational 00Z GFS and ECMWF\nalmost completely out of phase with each other in the western U.S.\nThe GFS shows a 550 dm 500 mb low over Nevada with a broad trough\ndown toward the Mojave and Sonoran Deserts Thursday evening, \nwhile the ECMWF shows ridging from the Guadalupe Mountains of \nsouthwest Texas up toward Nevada. With that, the GFS shows \nwidespread precipitation potential across the area Thursday and \nFriday while the ECMWF shows little precipitation. Some of this \nis still just beyond the timeline of this forecast so we will have\nthe chance to see if models begin aligning themselves more. NBM \ndoes have precipitation chances Wednesday Night into Thursday, \nwhich seems reasonable enough to have some potential with the \ndifferences in the model solutions, but this forecast will \ncertainly evolve.",
